WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.840
If you have a use case for generative AI,

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:07.520
how do you decide on which&nbsp;
foundation model to pick to run it?

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:11.600
With the huge number of&nbsp;
foundation models out there,

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:12.880
It's not an easy question.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.000
Different models are trained on different&nbsp;
data and have different parameter counts,

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.440
and picking the wrong model can&nbsp;
have severe unwanted impact,

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:25.440
like biases originating from the training data&nbsp;
or hallucinations that are just plain wrong.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.320
Now, one approach is to just pick the largest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:33.920
most massive model out&nbsp;
there to execute every task.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.720
The largest models have huge parameter counts

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:42.480
and are usually pretty good generalists,&nbsp;
but with large models come costs,

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.160
costs of compute, cost of&nbsp;
complexity and costs of variability.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:52.400
So often the better approach is to pick the right&nbsp;
size model for the specific use case you have.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.280
So let me propose to you an&nbsp;
AI model selection framework.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.880
It has six pretty simple stages.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:06.200
Let's take a look at what they areand then&nbsp;
give some examples of how this might work.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:11.800
Now, stage one, that is to&nbsp;
clearly articulate your use case.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:16.280
What exactly are you planning&nbsp;
to use generative A.I. for?

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.680
From there you'll list some of the&nbsp;
model options available to you.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:25.440
Perhaps there are already a subset of foundation&nbsp;
models running that you have access to.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:29.840
With a short list of models you'll next&nbsp;
want to identify each model's size,

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.680
performance costs, risks, and deployment methods.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:39.560
Next, evaluate those model characteristics&nbsp;
for your specific use case.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:40.800
Run some tests.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.520
That's the next stage,

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:46.640
testing options based on your previously&nbsp;
identified use case and deployment needs.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.360
And then finally, choose the option&nbsp;
that provides the most value.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.560
So let's put this framework to the test.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:02.080
Now, my use case, we're going to say&nbsp;
that is a use case for text generation.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:07.640
I need the AI to write personalized&nbsp;
emails for my awesome marketing campaign.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:09.440
That's stage one.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.600
Now, my organization is already using&nbsp;
two foundation models for other things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.400
so I'll evaluate those.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:19.680
First of all, we've got Llama 2

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:26.760
and specifically the Llama 2 70 model. a&nbsp;
fairly large model, 70 billion parameters.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:31.640
It's from meta and I know it's quite&nbsp;
good at some text generation use cases.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:36.160
Then there's also Granite that we have deployed.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.920
Granite is a smaller general&nbsp;
purpose model and that's from IBM.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.680
And I know there is a 13 billion parameter model

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:48.200
that I've heard does quite well&nbsp;
with text generation as well.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:54.080
So those are the models I'm going&nbsp;
to evaluate, Llama 2 and Granite.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.600
Next, we need to evaluate model&nbsp;
size, performance, and risks.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:04.960
And a good place to start&nbsp;
here is with the model card.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:08.320
The model cards might tell&nbsp;
us if the model has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.400
trained on data specifically for our purposes.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.360
Pre-trained Foundation models are&nbsp;
fine tuned for specific use cases

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:21.200
such as sentiment analysis or document&nbsp;
summarization or maybe text generation.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.840
And that's important to know&nbsp;
because if a model is pre trained

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:29.480
on a use case close to ours, it may&nbsp;
perform better when processing our prompts

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:34.160
and enable us to use zero shot&nbsp;
prompting to obtain our desired results.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.760
And that means we can simply&nbsp;
ask the model to perform tasks

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.200
without having to provide&nbsp;
multiple completed examples first.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:48.880
Now, when it comes to evaluating model performance&nbsp;
for our use case, we can consider three factors.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:53.960
The first factor that we&nbsp;
would consider is accuracy.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:58.120
Now, accuracy denotes how close&nbsp;
the generated output is to the

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:03.040
desired output, and it can be measured
objectively and repeatedly

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:06.720
by choosing evaluation metrics that&nbsp;
are relevant to your use cases.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:11.720
So for example, if your use case&nbsp;
related to text translation,

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:18.560
the B.L.E.U. - that's the BiLingual&nbsp;
Evaluation Understudy benchmark,

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:23.080
can be used to indicate the quality&nbsp;
of the generated translations.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:29.680
Now the second factor relates&nbsp;
to reliably of the model.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:33.800
Now that's a function of several&nbsp;
factors actually, such as consistency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.960
explainability and trustworthiness,

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:40.160
as well as how well a model&nbsp;
avoids toxicity like hate speech.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:42.600
Reliability comes down to trust,

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:46.880
and trust is built through transparency&nbsp;
and traceability of the training data

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:50.400
and accuracy and reliability of the output.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.080
And then the third factor that is speed.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:56.840
And specifically we're saying

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:00.440
how quickly does a user get a&nbsp;
response to a submitted prompt?

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:05.000
Now, speed and accuracy&nbsp;
are often a trade off here.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:09.080
Larger models may be slower, but&nbsp;
perhaps deliver a more accurate answer.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:12.440
Or then again, maybe the smaller model is faster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:15.520
and has minimal differences in&nbsp;
accuracy to the larger model.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:20.960
It really comes down to finding the sweet&nbsp;
spot between performance, speed and cost.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.240
A smaller, less expensive model may not offer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:27.440
performance or accuracy metrics&nbsp;
on par with an expensive one, but

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:30.000
it would still be preferable over the latter.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.920
If you consider any additional benefits,&nbsp;
the model might deliver like lower latency

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:37.800
and greater transparency into&nbsp;
the model inputs and outputs.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:41.000
The way to find out is to simply&nbsp;
select the model that's likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:46.120
to deliver the desired output and well, test it.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:49.920
Test that model with your&nbsp;
prompts to see if it works,

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:54.960
and then assess the model, performance&nbsp;
and quality of the output using metrics.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:58.680
Now, I've mentioned deployment in&nbsp;
passing, so a quick word on that.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:05.480
As a decision factor, we need to evaluate where&nbsp;
and how we want the model and data to be deployed.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:09.920
So let's say that we're leaning towards
Llama 2

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:14.640
as our chosen model based on our testing.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.080
Right, cool. Llama 2.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:20.840
That's an open source model and we could&nbsp;
inference with it on a public cloud.

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:24.800
So we've got a public cloud already out here.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:31.600
It's got an element of choice in it, which&nbsp;
is limited to we can just inference to that.

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:36.560
But if we decide we want to fine tune&nbsp;
the model with our own enterprise data,

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:40.560
we might need to deploy it on prem.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:47.120
So this is where we have&nbsp;
our own version of Llama two

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:50.600
and we are going to provide fine tuning to it.

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:53.840
Now, deploying on premise&nbsp;
gives you greater control,

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:57.280
and more security benefits compared&nbsp;
to a public cloud environment.

00:06:57.280 --> 00:06:59.840
But it's an expensive proposition,

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:03.320
especially when factoring&nbsp;
model size and compute power,

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:07.800
including the number of GPUs it takes
to run a single large language model.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:12.440
Now, everything we've discussed&nbsp;
here is tied to a specific use case,

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:17.760
but of course it's quite likely that any given&nbsp;
organization will have multiple use cases.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:21.360
And as we run through this&nbsp;
model selection framework,

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:26.720
we might find that each use case is better&nbsp;
suited to a different foundation model.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:29.440
That's called a multi model approach.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:35.320
Essentially, not all A.I. models are the&nbsp;
same, and neither are your use cases.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:39.960
And this framework might be just&nbsp;
what you need to pair the models

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:54.080
and the use cases together to find&nbsp;
a winning combination of both.

